# node.env.example — the declared environment of ONE node.
# Copy to ~/.config/code-factory/<project>.node.env on the control host and name it as
# NODE_ENV_FILE in that project's bunker config (config/bunker.example.conf).
#
# scripts/node_env.py applies it ON the node, before the harness layer starts a gateway,
# and it can be re-applied at any time: --check prints what drifted, --apply closes it.
#
# Grammar: one declaration per line, comments with '#', nothing else is accepted. A value is
# everything after the first '=' — no inline comments, no quoting rules.
#
#   ENV_<NAME>=<value>   the literal value goes into ~/.hermes/.env
#   ENV_<NAME>=@<path>   the value is READ FROM <path> on the node (a leading ~/ is the
#                        node's own $HOME)
#
# A name matching KEY|TOKEN|SECRET|PASS must use the @<path> form. This file is committed,
# copied to the node and quoted in diffs, so a literal secret in it is a leaked credential;
# node_env.py refuses such a line by name and never echoes the value.

# The restricted inference proxy's key — the one variable a node actually needs in .env.
# The file is placed on the node out of band at mode 600; this profile names it and never
# carries it.
ENV_CF_PROXY_API_KEY=@~/.node/auth/provider.key

# Everything else a node needs is a scalar in ~/.hermes/config.yaml, declared in the
# provisioner config rather than here. The exact spellings (and the only paths allowed):
#   CONFIG_SET_MODEL_PROVIDER=cf-proxy         -> model.provider
#   CONFIG_SET_MODEL_NAME=<model-id>           -> model.name
#   CONFIG_SET_GATEWAY_BIND=0.0.0.0            -> gateway.bind
#   CONFIG_SET_GATEWAY_PORT=10800              -> gateway.port
#   CONFIG_SET_AGENT_MAX_ITERATIONS=40         -> agent.max_iterations
# Any other path is refused by name: a profile owns only the keys the harness reads, and
# guessing a key is how a node ends up with a setting nothing honours.
